INT. VOSSBERG & CRANE - EXECUTIVE NAP POD ROOM - DAY

Six sleek pods, each shaped like a lacquered egg. Soft ambient chimes. A sign on the wall: REST IS RESULTS.

PRUDENCE ADEYEMI, 40s, in a pressed navy suit, stands outside pod three. She has been standing there a long time. Her phone is in her hand, screen dark.

MALCOLM VOSSBERG, 50s, in shirtsleeves, tie loosened to a politician's disarray, emerges from pod four rubbing his eyes. He sees her. He smiles. He is not surprised.

MALCOLM
Prudence. I thought you'd gone home.

PRUDENCE
I did.

MALCOLM
Then you came back.

PRUDENCE
For my coat.

She doesn't move toward the coat rack. He does. He lifts her coat off a hook, holds it out. She takes it. Doesn't put it on.

MALCOLM
Rough week.

PRUDENCE
It's Thursday.

MALCOLM
Rough Thursday.

He adjusts a pod's dial. The chimes change key.

MALCOLM (CONT'D)
The numbers will be fine. They always are.

PRUDENCE
Are they?

MALCOLM
You doubt me?

PRUDENCE
I doubt the spreadsheet.

MALCOLM
It's your spreadsheet.

PRUDENCE
It's the one you rewrote.

Silence. The chimes continue. A faint hiss of oxygen from the pods.

MALCOLM
I thought you'd appreciate the efficiency.

PRUDENCE
Efficiency.

MALCOLM
Streamlining.

Prudence folds her coat over her arm. Folds it a second time. Smooths it flat with both hands, too carefully.

PRUDENCE
I'll be in my office.

MALCOLM
You were just leaving.

PRUDENCE
I changed my mind.

He nods. Looks at the pod. Looks at her.

MALCOLM
Sleep on it. That's what these are for.

She turns, walks to the door. Pauses with one hand on the frame.

PRUDENCE
Do you sleep in there?

MALCOLM
Every day.

PRUDENCE
And dream?

MALCOLM
Never.

PRUDENCE
That's what I thought.

She leaves. He watches the door close, then climbs back into pod four and seals the lid.
